def get_part1_raw():
    return [{'q_num': 1, 'q': 'Plant growth is unique among multicellular organisms because it is generally:', 'opts': {'A': 'Open and indeterminate', 'B': 'Closed and determinate', 'C': 'Restricted to early embryonic stages', 'D': 'Non-cellular'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': "Plant growth is considered 'open' because new cells are constantly being added to the plant body throughout its life by the persistent activity of meristems.", 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Characteristics of Growth', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 2, 'q': 'The perpetual ability of plants to grow throughout life is primarily due to the presence of:', 'opts': {'A': 'Meristems at specific locations', 'B': 'Permanent dead sclerenchyma', 'C': 'Continuous photosynthetic pigments', 'D': 'High internal water pressure alone'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Meristems possess cells with the capacity for continuous self-perpetuation and division, maintaining the indeterminate open growth of plants.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Characteristics of Growth', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 3, 'q': 'Primary growth that elongates plant axes along their longitudinal axis is mainly contributed by:', 'opts': {'A': 'Root and shoot apical meristems', 'B': 'Cork cambium', 'C': 'Vascular cambium', 'D': 'Interfascicular cambium'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Apical meristems of roots and shoots are responsible for primary growth, promoting the elongation of the plant axis along its length.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Primary vs Secondary Growth', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 4, 'q': 'Secondary growth in gymnosperms and dicotyledonous plants increases axis girth and is driven by:', 'opts': {'A': 'Lateral meristems (vascular cambium and cork cambium)', 'B': 'Apical meristems only', 'C': 'Intercalary meristems only', 'D': 'Dermal stomatal guard cells'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Lateral meristems, namely vascular cambium and cork cambium (phellogen), appear later in development and increase the girth/diameter of the organs.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Primary vs Secondary Growth', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 5, 'q': 'At the cellular level, growth is essentially an irreversible permanent increase in:', 'opts': {'A': 'Size of an organ, part, or individual cell accompanied by metabolic energy expenditure', 'B': 'Temporary water uptake into the vacuole reversible by drought', 'C': 'Number of chloroplasts during bright sunshine only', 'D': 'Total volume of dead xylem sap without mass increase'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Growth is defined as an irreversible permanent increase in the size of an organ or its parts, or even of an individual cell, accompanied by metabolic processes.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Definition of Growth', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 6, 'q': 'According to NCERT data, a single maize root apical meristem can give rise to more than how many new cells per hour?', 'opts': {'A': '17,500 new cells per hour', 'B': '350 new cells per hour', 'C': '1,000 new cells per hour', 'D': '350,000 new cells per hour'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'NCERT explicitly notes that a single maize root apical meristem can produce more than 17,500 new cells per hour, illustrating growth as an increase in cell number.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Measurement of Growth', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 7, 'q': 'Cells in a watermelon fruit can increase in size/volume by up to how many times?', 'opts': {'A': '350,000 times', 'B': '17,500 times', 'C': '1,000 times', 'D': '50,000 times'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Cells in a watermelon fruit can enlarge up to 350,000 times their initial size, demonstrating growth as an increase in cell size/volume.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Measurement of Growth', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 8, 'q': 'The growth of a dorsiventral leaf is most appropriately measured in terms of increase in its:', 'opts': {'A': 'Surface area', 'B': 'Length alone', 'C': 'Cell diameter', 'D': 'Volume of xylem vessels'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Increase in surface area is the standard parameter used to measure the growth of broad flat dorsiventral leaves.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Measurement of Growth', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 9, 'q': 'Which parameter is universally considered the most reliable indicator of actual plant growth and dry matter accumulation?', 'opts': {'A': 'Increase in dry weight', 'B': 'Increase in fresh weight', 'C': 'Increase in cell turgor', 'D': 'Number of stomata'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Dry weight measurement eliminates fluctuations due to tissue water hydration, providing the most accurate estimate of actual biomass synthesis.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Measurement of Growth', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 10, 'q': 'Growth of a pollen tube through the style towards the ovary is measured in terms of its:', 'opts': {'A': 'Length', 'B': 'Surface area', 'C': 'Volume', 'D': 'Cell number'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Growth of a pollen tube is linear and is measured in terms of increase in length.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Measurement of Growth', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 11, 'q': 'The period of plant growth is traditionally divided into three successive phases. What is the correct spatial sequence from the root tip moving upwards?', 'opts': {'A': 'Meristematic phase -> Elongation phase -> Maturation phase', 'B': 'Maturation phase -> Elongation phase -> Meristematic phase', 'C': 'Elongation phase -> Meristematic phase -> Maturation phase', 'D': 'Meristematic phase -> Maturation phase -> Elongation phase'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Moving proximally from the growing root apex, the three successive zones are: meristematic zone, elongation zone, and maturation zone.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Phases of Growth', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 12, 'q': 'Cells in the meristematic zone are cytologically characterized by:', 'opts': {'A': 'Dense protoplasm, large conspicuous nuclei, thin primary cellulosic walls, and abundant plasmodesmata', 'B': 'Large central vacuoles and highly lignified thick secondary walls', 'C': 'Complete absence of nuclei and cytoplasm', 'D': 'Presence of root hairs on their outer surface'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Meristematic cells are actively dividing and have rich protoplasm, prominent nuclei, thin primary cell walls with plasmodesmatal interconnections, and lack secondary thickening.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Phases of Growth', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 13, 'q': 'Which phase of growth immediately follows the meristematic phase and is characterized by increased vacuolation and rapid cell enlargement?', 'opts': {'A': 'Phase of elongation', 'B': 'Phase of maturation', 'C': 'Phase of senescence', 'D': 'Dormancy phase'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'The elongation phase, located just proximal to the meristematic apex, exhibits rapid cell enlargement, new cell wall deposition, and vacuolation.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Phases of Growth', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 14, 'q': 'In which phase of growth do plant cells attain their maximal size in terms of wall thickening and protoplasmic modifications?', 'opts': {'A': 'Phase of maturation', 'B': 'Phase of elongation', 'C': 'Phase of cell division', 'D': 'Meristematic phase'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'In the maturation zone, cells undergo final structural and physiological differentiation, attaining their maximal size and cell wall specialization.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Phases of Growth', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 15, 'q': 'Root hairs that absorb water and minerals from the soil emerge exclusively from which zone of the root?', 'opts': {'A': 'Zone of maturation', 'B': 'Zone of elongation', 'C': 'Meristematic zone', 'D': 'Root cap'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Epidermal cells in the zone of maturation differentiate to form fine, delicate tubular root hairs.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Phases of Growth', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 16, 'q': 'Growth rate is defined mathematically as:', 'opts': {'A': 'Increased growth per unit time', 'B': 'Total weight divided by plant height', 'C': 'Volume divided by surface area', 'D': 'Number of leaves produced per year'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Growth rate is the increased growth (in length, mass, area, or cell number) per unit time.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Growth Rates', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 17, 'q': 'In arithmetic growth, following mitotic cell division:', 'opts': {'A': 'Only one daughter cell continues to divide while the other differentiates and matures', 'B': 'Both daughter cells continue to divide indefinitely', 'C': 'Neither daughter cell can divide again', 'D': 'All daughter cells undergo meiosis immediately'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'In arithmetic growth, mitosis yields one cell that remains meristematic while the other daughter cell undergoes differentiation and stops dividing.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Arithmetic Growth', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 18, 'q': 'Which mathematical equation represents arithmetic growth?', 'opts': {'A': 'Lt = L0 + rt', 'B': 'W1 = W0 * e^rt', 'C': 'r = (ln W1 - ln W0) / t', 'D': 'Lt = L0 * r^t'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'The linear equation for arithmetic growth is Lt = L0 + rt, where Lt is length at time t, L0 is length at time zero, and r is the elongation/growth rate.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Arithmetic Growth', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 19, 'q': 'Plotting length against time in arithmetic growth produces which type of curve?', 'opts': {'A': 'A straight linear line', 'B': 'A sigmoid (S-shaped) curve', 'C': 'A hyperbolic curve', 'D': 'A bell-shaped Gaussian curve'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Because a constant increment of length is added per unit time, plotting Lt against time gives a straight linear graph.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Arithmetic Growth', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 20, 'q': 'In geometric growth, following mitotic cell division:', 'opts': {'A': 'Both daughter cells retain the capacity to divide', 'B': 'Only one daughter cell divides while the other dies', 'C': 'Both daughter cells immediately lose their nuclei', 'D': 'Cells divide only once and permanently arrest'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'In geometric growth, all progeny cells retain mitotic dividing capability, leading to exponential proliferation (1 -> 2 -> 4 -> 8 -> 16).', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Geometric Growth', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 21, 'q': 'The typical growth curve of cells, tissues, organs, and whole plants growing in a natural environment is:', 'opts': {'A': 'A sigmoid (S-shaped) curve', 'B': 'A straight horizontal line', 'C': 'A downward parabolic curve', 'D': 'An inverted U-shaped curve'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'A sigmoid or S-curve is characteristic of living organisms growing in a natural habitat where nutrient and space limitations eventually constrain exponential growth.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Sigmoid Curve', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 22, 'q': 'What are the three successive phases of a typical sigmoid growth curve?', 'opts': {'A': 'Lag phase -> Log (exponential) phase -> Stationary phase', 'B': 'Log phase -> Lag phase -> Stationary phase', 'C': 'Stationary phase -> Log phase -> Lag phase', 'D': 'Lag phase -> Stationary phase -> Log phase'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Growth starts slowly (lag phase), accelerates exponentially (log/exponential phase), and finally plateaus due to limiting factors (stationary phase).', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Sigmoid Curve', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 23, 'q': 'Which mathematical formula describes exponential / geometric growth?', 'opts': {'A': 'W1 = W0 * e^rt', 'B': 'Lt = L0 + rt', 'C': 'W1 = W0 + rt', 'D': 'e = W1 / W0'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Exponential growth is expressed as W1 = W0 * e^rt, where W1 is final size, W0 is initial size, r is relative growth rate, and t is time.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Geometric Growth', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 24, 'q': "In the geometric growth equation W1 = W0 * e^rt, what does 'r' represent?", 'opts': {'A': "Relative growth rate and measure of the plant's ability to produce new matter (efficiency index)", 'B': 'Radius of the stem axis', 'C': 'Rate of transpiration', 'D': 'Root length at time zero'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': "Here 'r' is the relative growth rate and is also termed the efficiency index, reflecting the plant's metabolic capacity to generate new biomass.", 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Geometric Growth', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 25, 'q': 'Measurement and comparison of total growth per unit time in an organ is termed:', 'opts': {'A': 'Absolute growth rate (AGR)', 'B': 'Relative growth rate (RGR)', 'C': 'Efficiency index', 'D': 'Specific growth rate'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Absolute growth rate is the total measurement and comparison of growth achieved per unit time (e.g. increase in area cm2/day).', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'AGR vs RGR', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 26, 'q': 'The process where cells derived from root and shoot apical meristems undergo structural modifications to perform specific functions and lose dividing capacity is called:', 'opts': {'A': 'Differentiation', 'B': 'Dedifferentiation', 'C': 'Redifferentiation', 'D': 'Senescence'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Differentiation is the maturation process in which newly formed cells undergo protoplasmic and wall changes to take on specific physiological functions.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Differentiation', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 27, 'q': 'During differentiation of a tracheary element (vessel or tracheid), what major structural change occurs?', 'opts': {'A': 'Cells lose their living protoplasm and develop strong, elastic lignocellulosic secondary cell wall thickenings', 'B': 'Cells divide into four daughter cells via meiosis', 'C': 'Cells develop chloroplasts to carry out photosynthesis', 'D': 'Cells develop flagella to move through the stem'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'To transport water under immense tension, tracheary elements lose their living protoplasm and synthesize robust, lignified secondary cell walls.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Differentiation', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 28, 'q': 'When fully differentiated, living parenchymatous cells regain the capacity to divide under certain conditions, this phenomenon is called:', 'opts': {'A': 'Dedifferentiation', 'B': 'Redifferentiation', 'C': 'Differentiation', 'D': 'Abscission'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Dedifferentiation is the process by which permanent living cells that have lost mitotic capacity regain dividing ability to form secondary meristems.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Dedifferentiation', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 29, 'q': 'Which of the following plant tissues is a classic example of a dedifferentiated meristem?', 'opts': {'A': 'Interfascicular cambium and cork cambium (phellogen)', 'B': 'Apical meristem of root', 'C': 'Apical meristem of shoot', 'D': 'Xylem vessels in wood'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Interfascicular cambium and cork cambium are formed by the dedifferentiation of fully differentiated medullary ray cells and cortical parenchyma.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Dedifferentiation', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 30, 'q': 'Cells produced by dedifferentiated meristems (such as secondary xylem and cork) that once again lose dividing capacity and mature are called:', 'opts': {'A': 'Redifferentiated cells', 'B': 'Dedifferentiated cells', 'C': 'Primary meristematic cells', 'D': 'Embryonic cells'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Redifferentiation is when cells produced by secondary (dedifferentiated) meristems lose the ability to divide and specialize for specific permanent functions.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Redifferentiation', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 31, 'q': 'Secondary xylem, secondary phloem, and phellem (cork) are products of which developmental process?', 'opts': {'A': 'Redifferentiation', 'B': 'Dedifferentiation', 'C': 'Primary differentiation', 'D': 'Apical cleavage'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Secondary tissues produced by the activity of vascular cambium and cork cambium undergo redifferentiation to mature into permanent secondary elements.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Redifferentiation', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 32, 'q': "Development is broadly defined as the sum of which two continuous processes throughout a plant's lifecycle?", 'opts': {'A': 'Growth and Differentiation', 'B': 'Transpiration and Respiration', 'C': 'Mitosis and Meiosis', 'D': 'Absorption and Guttation'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Development includes all changes that an organism goes through during its lifecycle from germination of the seed to senescence; Development = Growth + Differentiation.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Development', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 33, 'q': 'The ability of plants to follow different pathways in response to environmental conditions or phases of life to form different structures is termed:', 'opts': {'A': 'Plasticity', 'B': 'Photoperiodism', 'C': 'Apical dominance', 'D': 'Vernalization'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Plasticity is the developmental adaptability of plants to produce distinct morphological forms in response to environmental cues or lifecycle stages.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Plasticity', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 34, 'q': 'The occurrence of different shapes of leaves on the same plant is known as:', 'opts': {'A': 'Heterophylly', 'B': 'Heterospory', 'C': 'Heterogamy', 'D': 'Heterotrophy'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Heterophylly is the phenomenon of producing different types/shapes of leaves on the same plant individual.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Plasticity', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 35, 'q': 'In cotton, coriander, and larkspur, heterophylly is a manifestation of plasticity based on:', 'opts': {'A': 'Different phases of life (juvenile vs mature stage)', 'B': 'Terrestrial vs aquatic habitats', 'C': 'Sun vs shade exposure alone', 'D': 'Nitrogen availability in soil'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'In cotton, coriander, and larkspur, the leaves of juvenile plants are distinctly different in shape from those of mature plants, demonstrating developmental plasticity.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Plasticity', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 36, 'q': 'In buttercup (Ranunculus flabellaris), the leaves submerged in water are finely dissected while those in air are broad and lobed. This is an example of:', 'opts': {'A': 'Environmental plasticity', 'B': 'Nutritional deficiency', 'C': 'Viral infection', 'D': 'Permanent somatic mutation'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Ranunculus demonstrates environmental plasticity: aquatic submerged leaves are finely dissected to resist water currents, whereas aerial leaves are broad and intact.', 'topic': 'growth_phases_rates', 'sub': 'Plasticity', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 37, 'q': 'Plant Growth Regulators (PGRs) are diverse chemical substances that are classified into two broad groups based on their functions:', 'opts': {'A': 'Plant growth promoters and Plant growth inhibitors', 'B': 'Hydrophilic and Lipophobic hormones', 'C': 'Inorganic catalysts and Heavy metals', 'D': 'Structural proteins and Lipids'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'PGRs are divided into growth promoters (auxins, gibberellins, cytokinins) and growth inhibitors (abscisic acid, ethylene).', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'PGR Overview', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 38, 'q': 'Which group of PGRs predominantly promotes cell division, cell enlargement, pattern formation, tropic growth, and flowering?', 'opts': {'A': 'Auxins, Gibberellins, and Cytokinins (growth promoters)', 'B': 'Abscisic acid alone', 'C': 'Ethylene and Abscisic acid', 'D': 'Synthetic herbicides only'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Auxins, GAs, and cytokinins are designated as plant growth promoters as they stimulate vital growth and organogenic activities.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'PGR Overview', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 39, 'q': "Which plant growth regulator is commonly known as the 'stress hormone' and primarily acts as a growth inhibitor?", 'opts': {'A': 'Abscisic acid (ABA)', 'B': 'Auxin (IAA)', 'C': 'Gibberellic acid (GA3)', 'D': 'Kinetin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Abscisic acid (ABA) promotes stomatal closure under drought, inhibits seed germination, and mediates response to environmental stresses.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Abscisic Acid', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 40, 'q': 'Ethylene is unique among plant growth regulators because it is a:', 'opts': {'A': 'Simple gaseous hydrocarbon (C2H4)', 'B': 'Complex steroid hormone', 'C': 'Polypeptide protein', 'D': 'Secondary alkaloid'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Ethylene is the only naturally occurring gaseous plant hormone, with the simple formula CH2=CH2.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethylene', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 41, 'q': 'Who observed that the coleoptile tips of canary grass (Phalaris canariensis) bent towards unilateral light, initiating the discovery of auxins?', 'opts': {'A': 'Charles Darwin and his son Francis Darwin', 'B': 'F.W. Went', 'C': 'E. Kurosawa', 'D': 'F. Skoog'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Charles Darwin and Francis Darwin demonstrated that canary grass coleoptiles perceive unilateral illumination at the tip, transmitting an influence to the sub-apical zone.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Auxin Discovery', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 42, 'q': 'Auxin was first isolated from human urine and oat (Avena sativa) coleoptile tips by which Dutch scientist in 1928?', 'opts': {'A': 'F.W. Went', 'B': 'Charles Darwin', 'C': 'Fritz Lipmann', 'D': 'Hans Krebs'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'F.W. Went successfully isolated auxin from oat coleoptile tips using agar blocks in 1928.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Auxin Discovery', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 43, 'q': 'The standard classic bioassay used for quantitatively measuring auxin activity is the:', 'opts': {'A': 'Avena curvature test', 'B': 'Barley endosperm test', 'C': 'Chlorophyll retention test', 'D': 'Triple response test'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': "F.W. Went's Avena curvature test measures the degree of bending of decapitated oat coleoptiles in response to agar blocks containing auxin.", 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Auxin Bioassay', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 44, 'q': "The 'bakanae' (foolish seedling) disease of rice, which caused infected seedlings to grow excessively tall and sterile, led to the discovery of:", 'opts': {'A': 'Gibberellins', 'B': 'Auxins', 'C': 'Cytokinins', 'D': 'Abscisic acid'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'The foolish seedling disease of rice led E. Kurosawa (1926) to discover gibberellins produced by the infecting ascomycete fungus Gibberella fujikuroi.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellin Discovery', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 45, 'q': 'Which Japanese scientist reported the appearance of bakanae symptoms in healthy rice seedlings treated with sterile filtrates of the fungus Gibberella fujikuroi?', 'opts': {'A': 'E. Kurosawa (1926)', 'B': 'F.W. Went', 'C': 'F. Skoog', 'D': 'H.H. Cousins'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'E. Kurosawa demonstrated that the disease was caused by a fungal chemical substance, which was later crystallized as gibberellic acid by Yabuta and Sumiki.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellin Discovery', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 46, 'q': 'How many different gibberellins (GAs) have been isolated from fungi and higher plants?', 'opts': {'A': 'More than 100 (GA1, GA2, GA3, etc.)', 'B': 'Only 2', 'C': '5', 'D': 'Exactly 10'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'More than 100 distinct gibberellins have been identified from fungi and plants; GA3 was one of the first to be discovered and remains the most intensively studied.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellins', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 47, 'q': 'Which scientist and his coworkers observed that tobacco callus proliferated only if nutrient medium contained auxin plus extracts of vascular tissues, yeast extract, or coconut milk?', 'opts': {'A': 'F. Skoog and coworkers', 'B': 'F.W. Went', 'C': 'E. Kurosawa', 'D': 'Charles Darwin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Folke Skoog and his collaborators discovered that tobacco pith cultures required an additional factor to undergo division, leading to the discovery of cytokinins.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Cytokinin Discovery', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 48, 'q': 'Skoog and Miller crystallized the cytokinesis-promoting active substance from autoclaved herring sperm DNA and named it:', 'opts': {'A': 'Kinetin', 'B': 'Zeatin', 'C': 'Auxin', 'D': 'Gibberellin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Skoog and Miller (1955) isolated and crystallized kinetin (6-furfurylaminopurine), a modified adenine derivative, from autoclaved herring sperm DNA.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Cytokinin Discovery', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 49, 'q': 'Does kinetin occur naturally in plants?', 'opts': {'A': 'No, kinetin is not a natural plant hormone; zeatin is the naturally occurring plant cytokinin', 'B': 'Yes, kinetin is the primary hormone in all leaves', 'C': 'Yes, kinetin is extracted from tomato roots', 'D': 'Yes, kinetin is produced by chloroplasts'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Kinetin does not occur naturally in plants. The search for natural cytokinin-like substances led to the isolation of zeatin from corn kernels and coconut milk.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Cytokinins', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 50, 'q': 'Zeatin, the first naturally occurring cytokinin, was isolated from immature maize grains (corn kernels) by:', 'opts': {'A': 'Letham and Miller', 'B': 'F.W. Went', 'C': 'E. Kurosawa', 'D': 'Charles Darwin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'D.S. Letham (1963) isolated zeatin from Zea mays kernels and coconut endosperm.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Cytokinins', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 51, 'q': 'H.H. Cousins (1910) confirmed that a volatile substance emitted from ripened oranges accelerated the ripening of stored unripened bananas. This volatile substance was:', 'opts': {'A': 'Ethylene', 'B': 'Auxin', 'C': 'Gibberellic acid', 'D': 'Abscisic acid'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Cousins demonstrated that volatile gas from oranges caused rapid ripening of nearby green bananas, later identified as ethylene.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethylene Discovery', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 52, 'q': 'During the mid-1960s, three independent research groups purified three inhibitors named Inhibitor-B, Abscission II, and Dormin. Later, these were proven to be chemically identical to:', 'opts': {'A': 'Abscisic acid (ABA)', 'B': 'Indole-3-acetic acid (IAA)', 'C': 'Kinetin', 'D': 'Gibberellic acid'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Inhibitor-B, Abscission II, and Dormin were shown to be the same sesquiterpene compound, universally designated as Abscisic Acid (ABA).', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'ABA Discovery', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 53, 'q': 'Which of the following is a naturally occurring auxin synthesized by plants?', 'opts': {'A': 'Indole-3-acetic acid (IAA) and Indole-3-butyric acid (IBA)', 'B': 'Naphthalene acetic acid (NAA)', 'C': '2,4-Dichlorophenoxyacetic acid (2,4-D)', 'D': '2,4,5-Trichlorophenoxyacetic acid (2,4,5-T)'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'IAA and IBA are natural auxins isolated from plants, whereas NAA and 2,4-D are synthetic auxins.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Auxins', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 54, 'q': 'Which of the following is a widely used synthetic auxin?', 'opts': {'A': 'NAA (Naphthalene acetic acid) and 2,4-D', 'B': 'Indole-3-acetic acid (IAA)', 'C': 'Zeatin', 'D': 'Gibberellin GA3'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'NAA and 2,4-D are man-made synthetic auxins widely used in agriculture and horticulture.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Auxins', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 55, 'q': 'Which amino acid is the primary biochemical precursor for the biosynthesis of natural auxin (IAA)?', 'opts': {'A': 'Tryptophan', 'B': 'Methionine', 'C': 'Tyrosine', 'D': 'Glycine'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'L-Tryptophan is the aromatic amino acid precursor for the synthesis of indole-3-acetic acid (IAA) in plants, requiring zinc (Zn2+) as an essential cofactor.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Auxin Biosynthesis', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 56, 'q': 'The phenomenon where the growing apical bud suppresses the growth of nearby lateral (axillary) buds is known as:', 'opts': {'A': 'Apical dominance', 'B': 'Parthenocarpy', 'C': 'Bolting', 'D': 'Photoperiodism'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Apical dominance is the inhibition of axillary lateral bud outgrowth exerted by auxins produced in the growing shoot apex.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Apical Dominance', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 57, 'q': "In tea plantations and hedge-making, apical dominance is commercially overcome by 'decapitation' (removal of shoot tips). What is the morphological result?", 'opts': {'A': 'Vigorous branching and dense bushy growth of lateral shoots', 'B': 'Death of the entire tea bush', 'C': 'Complete suppression of all leaf production', 'D': 'Conversion of the plant into a climber'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Decapitation removes the source of apical auxin, releasing lateral buds from inhibition and promoting dense bushy lateral shoots for higher leaf yield.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Apical Dominance', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 58, 'q': 'Auxin application promotes root initiation in stem cuttings. This property is widely utilized for:', 'opts': {'A': 'Vegetative propagation in horticulture and gardening', 'B': 'Killing mature trees', 'C': 'Harvesting wheat grains', 'D': 'Preventing flowers from blooming'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Root-promoting formulations containing IBA or NAA are applied to stem cuttings to stimulate adventitious root formation in nursery plant propagation.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Auxin Functions', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 59, 'q': "How does auxin regulate leaf and fruit abscission throughout a plant's lifecycle?", 'opts': {'A': 'It prevents premature drop of young leaves and fruits, but promotes abscission of older mature leaves and fruits', 'B': 'It causes immediate dropping of all young leaves', 'C': 'It permanently prevents any leaf from ever falling', 'D': 'It has zero effect on abscission layers'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'High auxin in young organs prevents abscission zone formation; as organs age, declining auxin promotes abscission layer development.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Auxin Functions', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 60, 'q': 'The induction of seedless fruit development without prior fertilization is called parthenocarpy. Auxins are commercially sprayed to induce parthenocarpy in:', 'opts': {'A': 'Tomatoes', 'B': 'Wheat', 'C': 'Mustard', 'D': 'Corn'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Auxin sprays induce parthenocarpy (seedless fruit formation) in tomatoes and other horticultural crops.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Parthenocarpy', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 61, 'q': 'The synthetic auxin 2,4-D is widely used in agriculture as a selective herbicide because it:', 'opts': {'A': 'Selectively kills broadleaf dicotyledonous weeds without harming mature monocotyledonous cereal crops', 'B': 'Kills all plants indiscriminately', 'C': 'Kills only grasses while saving broadleaf weeds', 'D': 'Acts as a fertilizer that doubles weed growth'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': '2,4-D is a selective dicot weedicide widely used to prepare weed-free lawns and cereal (wheat/rice/maize) crop fields.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Auxin Herbicide', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 62, 'q': 'Auxins control cell division and promote which vascular tissue differentiation in stems?', 'opts': {'A': 'Xylem differentiation', 'B': 'Stomatal guard cell division', 'C': 'Cork formation exclusively', 'D': 'Pollen grain meiosis'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Auxins promote vascular cambium activity and specifically induce the differentiation of primary and secondary xylem elements.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Auxin Functions', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 63, 'q': 'All gibberellins isolated to date are chemically categorized as:', 'opts': {'A': 'Acidic diterpenes possessing a gibbane ring skeleton', 'B': 'Basic purine derivatives', 'C': 'Polypeptide hormones', 'D': 'Gaseous hydrocarbons'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Gibberellins are acidic diterpenoids containing an ent-gibberellane ring system; hence they are called gibberellic acids (GA).', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellin Chemistry', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 64, 'q': 'Application of gibberellins to grapevines commercially serves to:', 'opts': {'A': 'Increase the length of the grape stalks, preventing cluster crowding', 'B': 'Turn all grapes into lemons', 'C': 'Make the grapes sour and dry', 'D': 'Stop grape seeds from germinating in soil'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'GA sprays elongate the pedicels and cluster axis of grapes, allowing loose fruit spacing and larger berry development.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellin Applications', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 65, 'q': 'Spraying sugarcane crops with gibberellins increases stem length and can increase the yield of sugar by up to:', 'opts': {'A': '20 tonnes per acre', 'B': '2 tonnes per acre', 'C': '50 kg per acre', 'D': '100 tonnes per acre'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'NCERT notes that spraying sugarcane with gibberellins elongates the internodes, increasing the cane yield by as much as 20 tonnes per acre.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellin Applications', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 66, 'q': 'Gibberellins are sprayed on fruits like apples to:', 'opts': {'A': 'Elongate the fruit and improve its market shape', 'B': 'Cause premature rotting', 'C': 'Make apples drop before harvest', 'D': 'Prevent sugar accumulation'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'GA improves the shape and size of apples, promoting market desirability and delaying fruit senescence.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellin Applications', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 67, 'q': 'In the brewing industry, gibberellic acid is applied to germinating barley grains to accelerate which commercial process?', 'opts': {'A': 'Malting (by stimulating alpha-amylase synthesis)', 'B': 'Distillation of whisky', 'C': 'Fermentation of yeast', 'D': 'Filtration of beer'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'GA stimulates the aleurone layer of barley seeds to secrete alpha-amylase and proteases, speeding up the malting process for brewing.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellin Applications', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 68, 'q': 'Spraying juvenile conifers with gibberellins has what commercial silvicultural benefit?', 'opts': {'A': 'Hastens the maturity period, leading to early seed production', 'B': 'Prevents conifers from ever producing needles', 'C': 'Causes conifer wood to dissolve', 'D': 'Stops conifers from absorbing water'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'GA application induces early reproductive maturity in juvenile conifer saplings, accelerating breeding programs and seed harvest.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellin Applications', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 69, 'q': 'The phenomenon of internode elongation just prior to flowering in rosette plants like cabbage, beet, and carrots is called:', 'opts': {'A': 'Bolting', 'B': 'Twinning', 'C': 'Etiolation', 'D': 'Vernalization'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Bolting is the rapid elongation of internodes followed by flowering in rosette plants, which can be chemically induced by gibberellin treatment under non-inductive photoperiods.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Bolting', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 70, 'q': 'Naturally occurring cytokinins are predominantly synthesized in which regions of the plant?', 'opts': {'A': 'Root apices, developing shoot buds, and young developing fruits', 'B': 'Mature brown tree bark only', 'C': 'Dead heartwood vessels', 'D': 'Dry seed coat hairs'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Natural cytokinins are synthesized in actively dividing tissues: root apical meristems, developing shoot buds, and young fruits.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Cytokinins', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 71, 'q': 'Which physiological effect of cytokinins directly antagonizes the action of auxins on lateral buds?', 'opts': {'A': 'Overcoming apical dominance and promoting lateral shoot growth', 'B': 'Causing leaf fall', 'C': 'Promoting tap root elongation', 'D': 'Inhibiting cell division'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'While auxin suppresses lateral bud outgrowth (apical dominance), cytokinins promote lateral shoot growth, releasing buds from apical arrest.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Cytokinins', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 72, 'q': 'The delay of leaf senescence by cytokinin application, which maintains chlorophyll content and nutrient mobilization, is known as the:', 'opts': {'A': 'Richmond-Lang effect', 'B': 'Pasteur effect', 'C': 'Warburg effect', 'D': 'Emerson effect'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'The Richmond-Lang effect (1957) is the retardation of leaf senescence and degradation of chlorophyll and proteins induced by cytokinins.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Richmond-Lang Effect', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 73, 'q': 'In plant tissue culture, organogenesis can be controlled by manipulating the ratio of auxin to cytokinin. A high cytokinin to auxin ratio favors:', 'opts': {'A': 'Shoot differentiation', 'B': 'Root differentiation', 'C': 'Callus proliferation without organs', 'D': 'Total culture senescence'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Skoog and Miller demonstrated that a high cytokinin:auxin ratio induces shoot development, while a high auxin:cytokinin ratio stimulates root development.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Tissue Culture Organogenesis', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 74, 'q': 'Which amino acid is the primary precursor for the biosynthesis of the gaseous hormone ethylene?', 'opts': {'A': 'Methionine', 'B': 'Tryptophan', 'C': 'Lysine', 'D': 'Glutamine'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Ethylene is biosynthesized from the sulfur-containing amino acid L-methionine via S-adenosylmethionine (SAM) and 1-aminocyclopropane-1-carboxylic acid (ACC).', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethylene Biosynthesis', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 75, 'q': "The classic 'triple response' of dicot seedlings exposed to ethylene consists of:", 'opts': {'A': 'Inhibition of stem elongation, horizontal (diageotropic) growth, and axis swelling with apical hook exaggeration', 'B': 'Rapid vertical stem elongation, root hair loss, and white flowers', 'C': 'Loss of all leaves, stem thinning, and root death', 'D': 'Immediate seed germination, chlorosis, and root bursting'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Ethylene induces the diagnostic triple response in etiolated dicot seedlings: inhibition of elongation, horizontal growth, and radial swelling with an exaggerated apical hook.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethylene Triple Response', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 76, 'q': 'Ethylene induces a sharp surge in cellular respiration during fruit ripening, which is termed the:', 'opts': {'A': 'Respiratory climacteric', 'B': 'Pasteur surge', 'C': 'Warburg effect', 'D': 'Photorespiratory spike'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'The respiratory climacteric is a transient, sharp rise in respiration rate triggered by ethylene that accompanies fruit ripening in apples, bananas, and tomatoes.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Respiratory Climacteric', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 77, 'q': 'Which commercial chemical formulation is an aqueous solution that is readily absorbed and transported within the plant to release ethylene slowly?', 'opts': {'A': 'Ethephon (2-chloroethylphosphonic acid)', 'B': '2,4-D', 'C': 'Kinetin', 'D': 'Gibberellic acid'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Ethephon breaks down inside plant tissues at neutral or alkaline pH to release ethylene gas, making it the most widely used source of agricultural ethylene.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethephon', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 78, 'q': 'Ethephon is commercially sprayed in agricultural fields to promote:', 'opts': {'A': 'Fruit ripening in tomatoes and apples, and fruit thinning in cotton, cherry, and walnut', 'B': 'Weed growth in lawns', 'C': 'Drying up of water in roots', 'D': 'Prevention of all flowering'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Ethephon hastens fruit ripening, promotes abscission/thinning of excess young fruits, and increases female flower numbers in cucumbers.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethephon', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 79, 'q': 'In deep-water rice varieties, which hormone promotes rapid petiole and internode elongation to keep leaves above the rising floodwater?', 'opts': {'A': 'Ethylene', 'B': 'Abscisic acid', 'C': 'Cytokinin', 'D': 'Florigen'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Submergence triggers ethylene accumulation in deep-water rice stems, driving rapid internode elongation so leaves remain above water.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethylene Functions', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 80, 'q': 'Which hormone promotes the formation of root hairs and increases the absorption surface area of root systems?', 'opts': {'A': 'Ethylene', 'B': 'Abscisic acid', 'C': 'Gibberellin', 'D': 'Auxin alone'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Ethylene promotes root growth and root hair formation, significantly expanding the surface area for water and mineral uptake.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethylene Functions', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 81, 'q': 'Ethylene breaks seed and bud dormancy and initiates germination in:', 'opts': {'A': 'Peanut seeds and potato tubers', 'B': 'Orchid seeds', 'C': 'Dry wheat grains', 'D': 'Conifer cones'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Ethylene breaks seed dormancy in peanuts and promotes sprouting of resting potato tubers.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethylene Functions', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 82, 'q': 'Abscisic acid (ABA) is called the stress hormone because it stimulates rapid:', 'opts': {'A': 'Closure of stomata in epidermis during water stress / drought', 'B': 'Opening of stomata during drought', 'C': 'Absorption of extra water from dry air', 'D': 'Conversion of leaves into spines'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Under water deficit, roots synthesize ABA which travels via xylem to guard cells, stimulating K+ and water efflux and closing stomata to conserve water.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Abscisic Acid', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 83, 'q': 'Abscisic acid acts as a general metabolic antagonist to which plant growth promoter?', 'opts': {'A': 'Gibberellins (GAs)', 'B': 'Ethylene', 'C': 'Auxins', 'D': 'Carotenoids'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'ABA is antagonistic to gibberellins: while GA breaks dormancy and induces alpha-amylase to promote germination, ABA establishes dormancy and suppresses germination.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'ABA vs GA Antagonism', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 84, 'q': 'Why is seed dormancy induced by ABA biologically advantageous for wild plants?', 'opts': {'A': 'It helps seeds withstand desiccation and adverse seasonal conditions until favorable weather returns for seedling survival', 'B': 'It prevents seeds from ever growing into plants', 'C': 'It causes seeds to dissolve in soil', 'D': 'It turns seeds into poisonous stones'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'ABA-mediated dormancy ensures that seeds do not germinate prematurely under hostile conditions (e.g. freezing winter or arid summer) before optimal conditions arrive.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Seed Dormancy', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 85, 'q': 'Which PGR is biosynthetically derived from carotenoids / violaxanthin?', 'opts': {'A': 'Abscisic acid (ABA)', 'B': 'Indole-3-acetic acid (IAA)', 'C': 'Ethylene', 'D': 'Kinetin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'ABA is a 15-carbon sesquiterpenoid synthesized via the cleavage of C40 carotenoid precursors (violaxanthin and neoxanthin) in plastids.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'ABA Biosynthesis', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 86, 'q': 'The response of plants to the relative durations of day and night (light and dark periods) for the induction of flowering is termed:', 'opts': {'A': 'Photoperiodism', 'B': 'Vernalization', 'C': 'Phototropism', 'D': 'Plasticity'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Photoperiodism is the physiological response of an organism to the length of day or night, particularly regarding flowering.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Photoperiodism', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 87, 'q': 'Photoperiodism was first discovered in Maryland Mammoth tobacco (Nicotiana tabacum) by:', 'opts': {'A': 'Garner and Allard (1920)', 'B': 'Darwin and Darwin', 'C': 'Went and Thimann', 'D': 'Skoog and Miller'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'W.W. Garner and H.A. Allard discovered photoperiodism in a giant mutant variety of tobacco known as Maryland Mammoth.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Photoperiodism Discovery', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 88, 'q': 'Plants that require exposure to light for a period exceeding a well-defined critical photoperiod to flower are called:', 'opts': {'A': 'Long-day plants (LDP)', 'B': 'Short-day plants (SDP)', 'C': 'Day-neutral plants (DNP)', 'D': 'Short-night plants'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Long-day plants flower only when the daylength exceeds their critical photoperiod (e.g. spinach, radish, wheat, henbane).', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Photoperiodic Categories', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 89, 'q': 'Plants that flower only when the daily exposure to light is less than a critical duration are called:', 'opts': {'A': 'Short-day plants (SDP)', 'B': 'Long-day plants (LDP)', 'C': 'Day-neutral plants (DNP)', 'D': 'Evergreen plants'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Short-day plants flower only when daylength is less than the critical duration (e.g. tobacco, soybean, rice, Chrysanthemum, Xanthium).', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Photoperiodic Categories', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 90, 'q': 'Plants in which flowering is not regulated by photoperiod and occurs regardless of daylength are called:', 'opts': {'A': 'Day-neutral plants (DNP)', 'B': 'Long-day plants', 'C': 'Short-day plants', 'D': 'Obligate photoperiodic plants'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Day-neutral plants (e.g. tomato, cucumber, cotton, sunflower, pea) flower independently of daylength once vegetative maturity is reached.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Photoperiodic Categories', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 91, 'q': 'In short-day plants (SDP), what is the truly critical factor for the induction of flowering?', 'opts': {'A': 'An uninterrupted, continuous dark period exceeding the critical night length', 'B': 'A continuous flash of blue light at noon', 'C': 'Exposure to 24 hours of light', 'D': 'Total deprivation of soil minerals'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': "Short-day plants are more accurately 'long-night plants' because they require an uninterrupted dark period exceeding critical duration to flower.", 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Critical Night Length', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 92, 'q': 'If the long dark night of a Short-Day Plant (SDP) is briefly interrupted by a flash of red light (night break), what happens to flowering?', 'opts': {'A': 'Flowering is completely inhibited', 'B': 'Flowering occurs twice as fast', 'C': 'Plant produces giant leaves but no roots', 'D': 'Flowering is unaffected'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'A brief flash of red light during the dark period converts Pr to Pfr, resetting the biological clock and abolishing flowering in short-day plants.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Night Break Effect', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 93, 'q': 'Which organ of the plant acts as the primary site of perception of light/dark photoperiodic stimuli?', 'opts': {'A': 'Leaves', 'B': 'Shoot apical meristem', 'C': 'Root tips', 'D': 'Flower petals'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Leaves perceive the photoperiodic light signals and synthesize a mobile flowering signal that migrates to shoot apices.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Site of Perception', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 94, 'q': 'The hypothetical mobile hormonal signal synthesized in leaves under inductive photoperiods that migrates via phloem to shoot apices to induce flowering is called:', 'opts': {'A': 'Florigen', 'B': 'Auxin', 'C': 'Abscisin', 'D': 'Dormin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': "Mikhail Chailakhyan proposed 'florigen' as the mobile floral stimulus that migrates from photo-induced leaves to shoot meristems (now known to involve FT protein).", 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Florigen', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 95, 'q': 'The photoreceptor pigment protein responsible for perceiving red and far-red light signals in photoperiodism is:', 'opts': {'A': 'Phytochrome', 'B': 'Chlorophyll a', 'C': 'Carotenoid', 'D': 'Anthocyanin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Phytochrome is a chromoprotein (photoreceptor) existing in two photoreversible forms: red-absorbing Pr (660 nm) and far-red-absorbing Pfr (730 nm).', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Phytochrome', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 96, 'q': 'The active physiological form of phytochrome that absorbs far-red light at 730 nm is:', 'opts': {'A': 'Pfr', 'B': 'Pr', 'C': 'P680', 'D': 'P700'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Pfr (phytochrome far-red) is the biologically active form that absorbs far-red light and triggers downstream developmental responses.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Phytochrome', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 97, 'q': 'The promotion or acceleration of flowering by exposure of seeds or young plants to a period of low temperature is called:', 'opts': {'A': 'Vernalization', 'B': 'Photoperiodism', 'C': 'Dormancy', 'D': 'Senescence'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Vernalization is the quantitative or qualitative dependence on low temperature (chilling) to induce or promote flowering.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Vernalization', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 98, 'q': 'Which of the following cereal crops possesses both winter and spring varieties dependent on vernalization?', 'opts': {'A': 'Wheat, Barley, and Rye', 'B': 'Maize and Sorghum', 'C': 'Rice and Sugarcane', 'D': 'Millet and Pearl millet'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Wheat, barley, and rye have spring and winter varieties. Spring varieties are planted in spring and flower in summer; winter varieties planted in autumn require winter chilling to flower the next summer.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Winter vs Spring Cereals', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 99, 'q': 'What happens if a winter variety of wheat is planted in the spring instead of autumn?', 'opts': {'A': 'It will fail to flower or produce mature grains within the normal growing season', 'B': 'It will flower in 2 days', 'C': 'It will produce double the grain yield', 'D': 'It will turn into barley'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Without winter chilling (vernalization), winter wheat remains in a vegetative rosette state and fails to produce flowering ears before the season ends.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Winter Cereals', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 100, 'q': 'Biennial plants (e.g., sugarbeet, cabbage, carrot) are monocarpic plants that normally grow vegetatively in their first year and flower in their second year following winter chilling. What happens if they are treated with gibberellin in the first year?', 'opts': {'A': 'They undergo bolting and flower without needing winter cold exposure', 'B': 'They rot immediately', 'C': 'They lose their root system', 'D': 'They produce seeds without flowers'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Gibberellin application substitutes for the cold requirement in biennials, inducing rapid bolting and flowering in the first growing season.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Biennials and Bolting', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 101, 'q': 'What is the primary site of perception for cold temperature during vernalization?', 'opts': {'A': 'Shoot apical meristem or embryo of the seed', 'B': 'Mature leaf blade', 'C': 'Root hairs', 'D': 'Petals'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Unlike photoperiodism (perceived by leaves), vernalization cold stimuli are perceived by actively dividing cells of the shoot apical meristem or seed embryo.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Vernalization Perception', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 102, 'q': 'The hypothetical mobile cold-induced stimulus that travels from the meristem to promote flowering is named:', 'opts': {'A': 'Vernalin', 'B': 'Florigen', 'C': 'Auxin', 'D': 'Cytokinin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Vernalin is the hypothetical hormonal factor postulated by Melchers to mediate the vernalization response.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Vernalin', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 103, 'q': 'The state in which viable seeds fail to germinate even when provided with favorable environmental conditions (moisture, oxygen, warmth) is known as:', 'opts': {'A': 'Seed dormancy', 'B': 'Seed viability', 'C': 'Seed death', 'D': 'Scarification'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Seed dormancy is an innate block to germination in viable seeds, ensuring that germination is synchronized with favorable seasons.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Seed Dormancy', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 104, 'q': 'Which of the following internal factors is a common cause of seed dormancy?', 'opts': {'A': 'Impermeable hard seed coat, chemical inhibitors (ABA, phenolic acids), or immature embryo', 'B': 'Excessive glucose storage in cotyledons', 'C': 'Presence of too many root hairs', 'D': 'High internal ATP concentration'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Dormancy can be caused by hard seed coats impervious to water/gas, biochemical germination inhibitors like ABA, or morphologically immature embryos.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Seed Dormancy Causes', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 105, 'q': 'The mechanical or chemical abrasion of hard seed coats using knives, sandpaper, or strong sulfuric acid to break dormancy is termed:', 'opts': {'A': 'Scarification', 'B': 'Stratification', 'C': 'Vernalization', 'D': 'Decapitation'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Scarification refers to methods (abrasion, acid etching, nicking) that breach impermeable seed coats to allow water uptake.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Breaking Dormancy', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 106, 'q': 'The practice of exposing moist dormant seeds to low chilling temperatures (stratification) mimics natural winter to break dormancy by:', 'opts': {'A': 'Degrading germination inhibitors like ABA and promoting gibberellin synthesis', 'B': 'Freezing the seeds into ice crystals', 'C': 'Destroying the seed embryo permanently', 'D': 'Boiling the seed coat proteins'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Cold stratification promotes GA synthesis and breaks down ABA, relieving dormancy.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Stratification', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 107, 'q': 'Which plant growth regulator can be applied exogenously to overcome seed dormancy and stimulate germination?', 'opts': {'A': 'Gibberellic acid (GA3) and Cytokinins', 'B': 'Abscisic acid', 'C': '2,4-D', 'D': 'Naphthalene acetic acid'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Gibberellins stimulate the production of hydrolytic enzymes (like alpha-amylase) to break seed dormancy and initiate germination.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Breaking Dormancy', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 108, 'q': 'Which inorganic chemical compound is commonly used in agriculture to break seed dormancy?', 'opts': {'A': 'Nitrates (KNO3) and thiourea', 'B': 'Sodium chloride', 'C': 'Mercury chloride', 'D': 'Potassium cyanide'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Potassium nitrate (KNO3) and thiourea are classic chemicals applied to break seed dormancy in agricultural seed testing.', 'topic': 'photoperiodism_vernalization', 'sub': 'Breaking Dormancy', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 109, 'q': "Why is abscisic acid (ABA) often called the 'dormin' of seeds?", 'opts': {'A': 'Because it enforces and maintains seed dormancy until washed out or degraded', 'B': 'Because it turns seeds into dormitories for ants', 'C': 'Because it causes seeds to produce dormant flowers', 'D': 'Because it dissolves the seed coat'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'ABA was originally named dormin because it induces and preserves metabolic dormancy in seeds and overwintering buds.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Abscisic Acid', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 110, 'q': 'Which hormone stimulates cell division (cytokinesis) in plant tissues?', 'opts': {'A': 'Cytokinin', 'B': 'Abscisic acid', 'C': 'Ethylene', 'D': 'Gibberellin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Cytokinins (e.g. zeatin, kinetin) directly promote cytokinesis and cell division.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Cytokinins', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 111, 'q': 'Which hormone promotes fruit ripening and causes abscission of leaves and flowers?', 'opts': {'A': 'Ethylene', 'B': 'Cytokinin', 'C': 'Gibberellic acid', 'D': 'Auxin alone'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Ethylene is the primary accelerator of fruit ripening and leaf/flower senescence and shedding.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethylene', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 112, 'q': 'Which hormone prevents premature drop of fruits like apples and citrus?', 'opts': {'A': 'Auxin (e.g. NAA, 2,4-D in low concentrations)', 'B': 'Ethylene', 'C': 'Abscisic acid', 'D': 'Gibberellin GA1'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Low concentrations of auxins prevent premature fruit drop before harvest.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Auxins', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 113, 'q': 'Which hormone induces bolting in rosette plants under non-inductive conditions?', 'opts': {'A': 'Gibberellin', 'B': 'Auxin', 'C': 'Cytokinin', 'D': 'Ethylene'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Gibberellins induce internode elongation and bolting in rosette plants.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellins', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 114, 'q': 'Which hormone promotes female flower formation in cucumbers to increase yield?', 'opts': {'A': 'Ethylene', 'B': 'Abscisic acid', 'C': 'Gibberellin', 'D': 'Auxin antagonist'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Ethylene application (as Ethephon) increases the number of female flowers in monoecious cucumbers.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethylene', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 115, 'q': 'Which hormone is used to kill broadleaf dicot weeds in cereal fields?', 'opts': {'A': '2,4-D (synthetic auxin)', 'B': 'GA3', 'C': 'Kinetin', 'D': 'Zeatin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': '2,4-D is a selective dicot weedicide widely used in monocot grain fields.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Auxin Herbicide', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 116, 'q': 'Which hormone is applied to barley seeds to speed up malting in the brewing industry?', 'opts': {'A': 'Gibberellic acid (GA3)', 'B': 'Abscisic acid', 'C': 'Ethylene', 'D': 'Kinetin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Gibberellins accelerate malting by stimulating amylase production in barley.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellins', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 117, 'q': 'Which hormone promotes closing of stomata during drought?', 'opts': {'A': 'Abscisic acid (ABA)', 'B': 'Auxin', 'C': 'Cytokinin', 'D': 'Gibberellin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'ABA mediates stomatal closure to minimize water loss during transpiration under moisture stress.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Abscisic Acid', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 118, 'q': 'Which hormone overcomes apical dominance and stimulates the sprouting of lateral buds?', 'opts': {'A': 'Cytokinin', 'B': 'Auxin', 'C': 'Abscisic acid', 'D': 'Ethylene'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Cytokinins promote lateral bud outgrowth, counteracting apical dominance.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Cytokinins', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 119, 'q': 'Which hormone is responsible for phototropic bending of shoot tips towards light?', 'opts': {'A': 'Auxin', 'B': 'Gibberellin', 'C': 'Cytokinin', 'D': 'Abscisic acid'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Auxin redistributes towards the shaded side of shoot tips, stimulating greater elongation on the dark side and bending towards light.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Phototropism', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 120, 'q': 'Which hormone accelerates maturity in juvenile conifers to induce early seed production?', 'opts': {'A': 'Gibberellin', 'B': 'Auxin', 'C': 'Cytokinin', 'D': 'Abscisic acid'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Gibberellins shorten the juvenile vegetative phase in young conifers, promoting early cone/seed production.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellins', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 121, 'q': 'Which hormone promotes petiole elongation in deep-water rice to keep foliage above water?', 'opts': {'A': 'Ethylene', 'B': 'Abscisic acid', 'C': 'Cytokinin', 'D': 'Auxin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Ethylene promotes rapid petiole and internode elongation in flooded deep-water rice.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethylene', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 122, 'q': "Which hormone was discovered in association with the 'bakanae' disease of rice?", 'opts': {'A': 'Gibberellin', 'B': 'Auxin', 'C': 'Cytokinin', 'D': 'Ethylene'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Gibberellin was discovered by Kurosawa from the fungus Gibberella fujikuroi causing bakanae disease.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Gibberellins', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 123, 'q': 'Which hormone was first crystallized from autoclaved herring sperm DNA?', 'opts': {'A': 'Kinetin', 'B': 'Zeatin', 'C': 'Auxin', 'D': 'Gibberellin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Kinetin was crystallized from autoclaved herring sperm DNA by Miller and Skoog.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Cytokinins', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 124, 'q': 'Which hormone was isolated from the endosperm of Zea mays?', 'opts': {'A': 'Zeatin', 'B': 'Kinetin', 'C': 'Gibberellin', 'D': 'Auxin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Zeatin is the natural cytokinin isolated from corn (Zea mays) kernels.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Cytokinins', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}, {'q_num': 125, 'q': 'Which hormone was identified as a gaseous substance released from ripened oranges by Cousins?', 'opts': {'A': 'Ethylene', 'B': 'Auxin', 'C': 'Cytokinin', 'D': 'Gibberellin'}, 'ans': 'A', 'exp': 'Cousins confirmed that ethylene is a volatile gas released by ripe oranges that ripens green bananas.', 'topic': 'pgrs', 'sub': 'Ethylene', 'diff': 'Easy', 'qtype': 'Single Correct MCQ'}]
